[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
Below is a complete, copy‑and‑paste‑ready recipe for loading a **single‑file `.pmtiles` archive** into a MapLibre GL JS map.

---

## 1️⃣ Install the PMTiles helper library

```bash
npm i pmtiles          # or yarn add pmtiles
```

`pmtiles` ships a tiny protocol handler (`new pmtiles.Protocol()`) that makes a `pmtiles://` URL work with MapLibre.

---

## 2️⃣ Register the protocol **once** (before any map is created)

```js
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import * as pmtiles from 'pmtiles';
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';

// Create the protocol object
const pmtilesProtocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();

// Register it under the scheme name "pmtiles"
maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', pmtilesProtocol.tile);
```

> **Why “once”?**  
> `addProtocol` registers a global handler. Adding it inside a React component’s render loop or inside a `new maplibregl.Map()` call will register the same handler many times and can cause memory‑leaks or duplicate requests.

> **MapLibre v4+ note** – the `pmtilesProtocol.tile` shim already knows the new promise‑based signature (`async (params, abortController) => { … }`). You can use it unchanged on v3, v4, v5, or v6.

---

## 3️⃣ Define a source that points at the archive

### Vector tiles (most common)

```js
const style = {
  version: 8,
  sources: {
    // The key you’ll reference in layers
    myPmtiles: {
      type: 'vector',
      // The protocol prefix tells MapLibre to use the handler above
      url: 'pmtiles://https://my-cdn.example.com/tiles/world.pmtiles'
    }
  },
  layers: [
    // Example: draw water polygons from the "water" layer inside the archive
    {
      id: 'water',
      type: 'fill',
      source: 'myPmtiles',
      'source-layer': 'water',          // exact layer name inside the PMTiles file
      paint: { 'fill-color': '#a0c8f0' }
    },
    // Add more layers (roads, labels, etc.) that reference the same source
    {
      id: 'roads',
      type: 'line',
      source: 'myPmtiles',
      'source-layer': 'road',
      paint: { 'line-color': '#777', 'line-width': 1 }
    }
  ]
};
```

### Raster imagery (PNG/JPEG tiles)

```js
const style = {
  version: 8,
  sources: {
    satellite: {
      type: 'raster',
      url: 'pmtiles://https://my-cdn.example.com/imagery/sat.pmtiles',
      tileSize: 256               // optional, defaults to 256
    }
  },
  layers: [
    {
      id: 'sat',
      type: 'raster',
      source: 'satellite',
      paint: {}
    }
  ]
};
```

### Raster‑DEM (terrain) – e.g. Terrarium‑encoded elevation

```js
const style = {
  version: 8,
  sources: {
    terrain: {
      type: 'raster-dem',
      url: 'pmtiles://https://my-cdn.example.com/elevation/terrain.pmtiles',
      encoding: 'terrarium'   // or "mapbox"
    }
  },
  terrain: {
    source: 'terrain',
    exaggeration: 1.5
  }
};
```

> **Important:** Use **`url:`** (not `tiles:`).  
> The PMTiles protocol reads the archive header, extracts the correct `minzoom`/`maxzoom`, and returns a TileJSON for MapLibre. If you use `tiles: ['pmtiles://…/{z}/{x}/{y}']` you lose that header information and MapLibre will assume a default max‑zoom of 22, causing unnecessary empty‑tile requests.

---

## 4️⃣ Create the map

```js
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',          // id of the <div>
  style,                     // the object from step 3
  center: [0, 0],            // lon, lat
  zoom: 2
});
```

That’s it—MapLibre will now request individual tiles from the `.pmtiles` file using HTTP **Range** requests.

---

## 5️⃣ Hosting requirements (so the client can fetch the file)

| Requirement | What to check |
|-------------|----------------|
| **Range requests** | The server must respond to `Range: bytes=` headers and return `Accept-Ranges: bytes`. All major static hosts (AWS S3, Cloudflare R2, GitHub Pages, Netlify, Vercel, etc.) do this out of the box. |
| **CORS** | The response must include `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your domain) and `Access-Control-Allow-Headers: Range`. If you host on S3, set the bucket CORS policy accordingly. |
| **Cache headers** | For an immutable archive you can set `Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000`. CDNs will cache the range responses, giving you global low‑latency tile delivery. |
| **File size limits** | GitHub Pages limits a single file to 100 MiB; other hosts usually allow many gigabytes. Split the dataset into multiple PMTiles files if you exceed the limit. |

---

## 6️⃣ Common pitfalls & how to fix them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **Blank vector tiles** (no features) | Using `tiles:` instead of `url:` → MapLibre thinks the archive has zooms up to 22 and asks for tiles that don’t exist. | Switch to `url:` as shown above. |
| **`Failed to fetch` / CORS error** | Host does not send `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` or does not allow `Range` headers. | Add a CORS rule on the bucket / static host. |
| **`pmtilesProtocol.tile` returns empty data** (MapLibre shows gray background) | Using an old `pmtiles` < 3.0 that only supports the callback API while MapLibre v4+ expects a promise returning `{data}`. | Upgrade `pmtiles` (`npm i pmtiles@latest`). The built‑in shim works with all recent MapLibre versions. |
| **Large file never loads** | Server is gzipping the whole `.pmtiles` file. Range requests on a gzipped response break because the byte offsets no longer match the archive. | Disable gzip/deflate for `.pmtiles` files (set `Content-Encoding: identity`). |
| **Missing glyphs / sprites** | You added vector label layers but didn’t provide a `glyphs` or `sprite` URL in the style. | Add `"glyphs": "https://my-cdn.example.com/fonts/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf"` and `"sprite": "https://my-cdn.example.com/sprite"` (or use MapLibre’s default). |

---

## 7️⃣ Full minimal example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>PMTiles + MapLibre</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>#map {position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%;}</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  import * as maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js';
  import * as pmtiles from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/pmtiles@3.0.0/dist/index.min.js';

  // 1️⃣ Register protocol
  const protocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();
  maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', protocol.tile);

  // 2️⃣ Style that points at a PMTiles file
  const style = {
    version: 8,
    sources: {
      world: {
        type: 'vector',
        url: 'pmtiles://https://example.com/tiles/world.pmtiles'
      }
    },
    layers: [
      {
        id: 'water',
        type: 'fill',
        source: 'world',
        'source-layer': 'water',
        paint: { 'fill-color': '#a0c8f0' }
      },
      {
        id: 'land',
        type: 'fill',
        source: 'world',
        'source-layer': 'land',
        paint: { 'fill-color': '#f0e6d6' }
      }
    ]
  };

  // 3️⃣ Create the map
  new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style,
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the page in a browser, and you’ll see the vector data coming straight from `world.pmtiles` without any tile server.

---

## 8️⃣ TL;DR checklist

1. `npm i pmtiles` (or load via CDN).  
2. `maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', new pmtiles.Protocol().tile);`  
3. In the style, use a **vector**, **raster**, or **raster-dem** source with `url: 'pmtiles://https://…/myfile.pmtiles'`.  
4. Host the file on a static server that supports **Range** and **CORS**.  
5. Create the map – MapLibre does the rest.

Happy mapping! 🚀

## TL;DR  

MapLibre GL JS v4 (and later) no longer uses the **callback‑style** `addProtocol` that existed in v3.  
The handler must now be an **async function** (or a function that returns a Promise) whose resolved value is an object that contains a `data` property (the tile bytes) and, optionally, `cacheControl` and `expires`.  

```js
// v4+ signature
maplibregl.addProtocol('myproto', async (params, abortController) => {
  const response = await fetch(params.url, { signal: abortController.signal });
  if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`Tile fetch error ${response.status}`);
  const buffer = await response.arrayBuffer();          // or response.blob()
  return { data: buffer };                              // <-- must be wrapped
});
```

If you were doing something like this in v3:

```js
// v3 – **does not work** in v4+
maplibregl.addProtocol('myproto', (params, callback) => {
  fetch(params.url)
    .then(r => r.arrayBuffer())
    .then(buf => callback(null, buf))
    .catch(err => callback(err));
  return { cancel: () => {} };
});
```

you need to rewrite it to the new shape (or, when you are using the **PMTiles** library, simply keep the one‑liner `maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', protocol.tile)` – the library already ships a v4‑compatible shim).

Below is a step‑by‑step guide to fix the problem, plus a few gotchas that commonly trip people up after the upgrade.

---

## 1. What changed in MapLibre GL JS v4?

| v3 (callback) | v4+ (promise) |
|---------------|---------------|
| `addProtocol(name, (params, callback) => { … })` | `addProtocol(name, async (params, abortController) => { … })` |
| The handler **must call** `callback(err, data)` | The handler **must return** a Promise that resolves to `{ data, cacheControl?, expires? }` |
| The return value of the handler is an object with a `cancel` method (optional) | The return value is ignored; cancellation is handled via the `AbortController` passed as the second argument |
| `callback(null, ArrayBuffer)` → tile works | `return { data: ArrayBuffer }` → tile works |
| Returning a raw `ArrayBuffer`, `Blob`, `Response`, or `Uint8Array` is accepted (MapLibre extracts the body) | **Must be wrapped** in an object with a `data` key; returning a raw buffer results in an empty tile |

Because of this contract change, any code that still uses the old callback signature will silently return an empty tile (or a “blank” raster) – MapLibre thinks the handler succeeded but finds no `data` property.

---

## 2. Minimal fix for a custom handler

Below is a **drop‑in replacement** for the most common pattern: fetch a tile from a remote HTTP endpoint (or from a static file) and hand it to MapLibre.

```js
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

// Register the protocol once, before any MapLibre map is created.
maplibregl.addProtocol('mytiles', async (params, abortController) => {
  // `params.url` already contains the full URL after the protocol prefix.
  // Example: "mytiles://https://example.com/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf"
  // The library strips the "mytiles://" part and passes the rest as `params.url`.

  try {
    const response = await fetch(params.url, {
      // Abort if the map cancels the request (e.g. user pans away)
      signal: abortController.signal,
    });

    if (!response.ok) {
      // Throwing aborts the request and makes MapLibre treat it as a tile error.
      throw new Error(`Tile fetch failed: ${response.status} ${response.statusText}`);
    }

    // Choose the representation you need:
    //   - `arrayBuffer()` for vector tiles (pbf, mvt)
    //   - `blob()` for raster tiles (png, jpg)
    //   - `json()` for TileJSON (rare)
    const tileData = await response.arrayBuffer();

    // The contract: return an object with a `data` property.
    // Optional: `cacheControl` and `expires` can be set if you want to
    // override HTTP caching for this tile.
    return { data: tileData };
  } catch (err) {
    // Re‑throw to signal an error to MapLibre.
    // MapLibre will mark the tile as failed and will retry according to its
    // retry policy.
    throw err;
  }
});
```

### What you *don’t* need to do any more

* No `callback` argument.
* No manual `return { cancel: … }` – the `AbortController` does the cancellation.
* No special handling for `params` beyond reading `params.url`.

---

## 3. If you’re already using the **PMTiles** library – you don’t have to rewrite anything

The `pmtiles` NPM package ships a **v4‑compatible shim** called `Protocol.tile`.  
All you need is:

```js
import * as pmtiles from 'pmtiles';
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

const protocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();   // creates the handler
maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', protocol.tile); // works on v3, v4, v5, v6
```

*Why does this work?*  
`protocol.tile` detects whether the second argument is an `AbortController` (v4+) or a callback (v3) and adapts accordingly. It always resolves to `{ data: Uint8Array, cacheControl?, expires? }`, satisfying the new contract.

**If you are on an old `pmtiles` version (< 3.0.0)**, upgrade:

```bash
npm install pmtiles@latest
```

Older versions only exported a callback‑style handler, which will break on v4.

---

## 4. Full example – a MapLibre map that uses a custom protocol **and** a PMTiles source

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre + PMTiles (v4+) demo</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>#map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  import * as maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js';
  import * as pmtiles from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/pmtiles@3.0.0/dist/index.min.js';

  // ---- Register the PMTiles protocol (v4‑compatible) ----
  const pmProtocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();
  maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', pmProtocol.tile);

  // ---- (Optional) Register a *custom* protocol that fetches PNG tiles ----
  maplibregl.addProtocol('mytiles', async (params, abortController) => {
    const resp = await fetch(params.url, { signal: abortController.signal });
    if (!resp.ok) throw new Error(`Tile error ${resp.status}`);
    const blob = await resp.blob();               // raster tile
    const arrayBuffer = await blob.arrayBuffer(); // MapLibre expects raw bytes
    return { data: arrayBuffer };
  });

  // ---- Create the map ----
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // Vector tiles stored in a PMTiles file
        osm: {
          type: 'vector',
          url: 'pmtiles://https://example.com/planet.osm.pmtiles' // <-- note the `pmtiles://` prefix
        },
        // Raster tiles served via a custom protocol (just to illustrate)
        satellite: {
          type: 'raster',
          url: 'mytiles://https://example.com/sat/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
          tileSize: 256
        }
      },
      layers: [
        {
          id: 'water',
          type: 'fill',
          source: 'osm',
          'source-layer': 'water',
          paint: { 'fill-color': '#a0c8f0' }
        },
        {
          id: 'satellite',
          type: 'raster',
          source: 'satellite',
          paint: {}
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2
  });

  // Clean‑up (optional but good practice)
  map.on('remove', () => {
    maplibregl.removeProtocol('pmtiles');
    maplibregl.removeProtocol('mytiles');
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

*Key points in the example*

* The **PMTiles** source uses `url: 'pmtiles://https://…'`. The protocol handler reads the archive header, returns a TileJSON with the correct `minzoom`/`maxzoom`, and serves individual tiles via range requests.
* The **custom** raster source uses `mytiles://…`. The handler is the v4‑style async function that returns `{ data: … }`.
* Both protocols are registered **once**, before the map is instantiated. The registration is global, so any later map on the same page automatically inherits the handlers.

---

## 5. Common pitfalls after the upgrade

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| Tiles appear **blank** (vector) or **transparent** (raster) but no error in the console | Handler returns a raw `ArrayBuffer`/`Blob` instead of `{data: …}` | Wrap the payload in `{ data: buffer }`. |
| Console shows `Error: Tile fetch error: 404` even though the URL works in the browser | The handler is still using the old callback signature, so MapLibre never sees the error and treats the response as success → empty tile. | Convert to async function returning `{data}` or throw an error. |
| `AbortError: The user aborted a request.` appears repeatedly when panning quickly | Your handler ignored the `AbortController` and kept fetching tiles that are no longer needed, causing the browser to abort them. | Pass `signal: abortController.signal` to `fetch`. |
| CORS errors (`No 'Access-Control-Allow-Origin' header`) after moving the .pmtiles file to S3 | The host does not send CORS headers for **Range** requests. | Configure the bucket (or CDN) to return `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your domain) and `Access-Control-Allow-Headers: Range`. |
| The map keeps requesting zoom levels that don’t exist in the archive (over‑zoom) and you see a lot of empty tiles | You used `tiles: ['pmtiles://…/{z}/{x}/{y}']` instead of `url:`. | Use `url:` so the protocol can read the archive header and supply the correct `maxzoom`. |
| `maplibregl.addProtocol is not a function` | You are still importing the **v3** bundle of MapLibre (e.g. `maplibre-gl@3.x`). | Upgrade to `maplibre-gl@4` (or later) and import from the new package. |

---

## 6. Quick checklist to verify your migration

1. **Upgrade MapLibre**  
   ```bash
   npm install maplibre-gl@^4   # or yarn add maplibre-gl@^4
   ```
2. **Upgrade the PMTiles library** (if you use it)  
   ```bash
   npm install pmtiles@latest
   ```
3. **Replace every `addProtocol` callback** with the async version (or use the PMTiles shim).  
   - Ensure the function signature is `(params, abortController)`.
   - Return `{ data: … }`.
4. **Test a single tile** in the network tab: you should see a `Range` request that returns a `206 Partial Content` with `Content-Range: bytes …`.  
   - If you see a `200 OK` of the whole file, the host is not honoring range requests (or you used `tiles:` instead of `url:`).  
5. **Check CORS** – the response must include `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` and `Accept-Ranges: bytes`.  
6. **Verify the map style** – use `url:` for PMTiles sources, not `tiles:`.  
7. **Run the app** – open the console, look for any `TileError` messages. If you see “Tile data is empty”, you probably forgot the `{data}` wrapper.

---

## 7. Full reference – `addProtocol` signature (v4+)

```ts
/**
 * Register a custom protocol handler.
 *
 * @param name   The protocol name (e.g. "pmtiles", "mytiles").
 * @param handler  An async function that receives:
 *   - params: { url: string, ... }   // the full URL after the protocol prefix
 *   - abortController: AbortController // can be used to cancel the fetch
 *
 * The handler must **return** a Promise that resolves to an object:
 *   {
 *     data: Array
